/* NCERT Biology Class XI — Chapter 2: Biological Classification (book page 10–21) */
addChapter({
  id: 'bot-11-02',
  subj: 'bot',
  cls: 11,
  no: 2,
  title: 'Biological Classification',
  src: 'NCERT Biology XI · Chapter 2',
  points: [
    { id: 'in1', topic: 'Introduction', t: 'Early attempts to classify were instinctive, based on uses (food, shelter, clothing). Aristotle was the earliest to attempt a more scientific basis — he classified plants into trees, shrubs and herbs, and animals into those with red blood and those without.' },
    { id: 'in2', topic: 'Two kingdom system', t: 'In Linnaeus\' time a Two Kingdom system (Plantae and Animalia) was developed. It did not distinguish between eukaryotes and prokaryotes, unicellular and multicellular organisms, and photosynthetic (green algae) and non-photosynthetic (fungi) organisms.' },
    { id: 'in3', topic: 'Two kingdom system', t: 'A large number of organisms did not fall into either category, so the two kingdom classification was found inadequate. Besides gross morphology, characters like cell structure, nature of wall, mode of nutrition, habitat, reproduction and evolutionary relationships were needed.' },
    { id: 'wh1', topic: 'Five kingdom classification', t: 'R.H. Whittaker (1969) proposed a Five Kingdom Classification: Monera, Protista, Fungi, Plantae and Animalia. Main criteria: cell structure, body organisation, mode of nutrition, reproduction and phylogenetic relationships.' },
    { id: 'wh2', topic: 'Three-domain system', t: 'The three-domain system divides Kingdom Monera into two domains, leaving the remaining eukaryotic kingdoms in the third domain — thereby a six kingdom classification.' },
    { id: 't21a', topic: 'Table 2.1', t: 'Cell type: Monera prokaryotic; Protista, Fungi, Plantae, Animalia eukaryotic. Nuclear membrane absent only in Monera.' },
    { id: 't21b', topic: 'Table 2.1', t: 'Cell wall: Monera non-cellulosic (polysaccharide + amino acid); Protista present in some; Fungi present with chitin; Plantae present (cellulose); Animalia absent.' },
    { id: 't21c', topic: 'Table 2.1', t: 'Body organisation: Monera and Protista cellular; Fungi multicellular/loose tissue; Plantae tissue/organ; Animalia tissue/organ/organ system.' },
    { id: 't21d', topic: 'Table 2.1', t: 'Mode of nutrition: Monera autotrophic (chemosynthetic and photosynthetic) and heterotrophic (saprophytic/parasitic); Protista autotrophic (photosynthetic) and heterotrophic; Fungi heterotrophic (saprophytic/parasitic); Plantae autotrophic (photosynthetic); Animalia heterotrophic (holozoic/saprophytic etc.).' },
    { id: 'old1', topic: 'Earlier classification issues', t: 'Earlier \'Plants\' included bacteria, blue green algae, fungi, mosses, ferns, gymnosperms and angiosperms — unified only by having a cell wall. It put prokaryotic bacteria and blue green algae with eukaryotes, and unicellular Chlamydomonas with multicellular Spirogyra under algae.' },
    { id: 'old2', topic: 'Earlier classification issues', t: 'It did not differentiate heterotrophic fungi from autotrophic green plants, though fungi had chitin in their walls while green plants had a cellulosic cell wall. So fungi were placed in a separate Kingdom Fungi.' },
    { id: 'old3', topic: 'Changes in criteria', t: 'All prokaryotes were grouped under Kingdom Monera and unicellular eukaryotes in Kingdom Protista. Protista brought together Chlamydomonas, Chlorella (earlier in Algae, with cell walls) with Paramoecium and Amoeba (earlier in animal kingdom, lacking cell wall) — because criteria for classification changed.' },
    { id: 'old4', topic: 'Changes in criteria', t: 'Such changes will occur in future too. The attempt has been to evolve a classification reflecting morphological, physiological and reproductive similarities and also phylogenetic (evolutionary) relationships.' },
    { id: 'mo1', topic: '2.1 Kingdom Monera', t: 'Bacteria are the sole members of Kingdom Monera and the most abundant micro-organisms. Hundreds of bacteria are present in a handful of soil; they live in extreme habitats like hot springs, deserts, snow and deep oceans; many are parasites.' },
    { id: 'mo2', topic: 'Bacterial shapes', t: 'Four categories by shape (Fig 2.1): spherical Coccus (cocci), rod-shaped Bacillus (bacilli), comma-shaped Vibrium (vibrio) and spiral Spirillum (spirilla).' },
    { id: 'mo3', topic: 'Bacterial metabolism', t: 'Bacterial structure is very simple but behaviour very complex; bacteria show the most extensive metabolic diversity. Some are autotrophic (photosynthetic or chemosynthetic); the vast majority are heterotrophs.' },
    { id: 'ar1', topic: '2.1.1 Archaebacteria', t: 'Archaebacteria live in harsh habitats: extreme salty areas (halophiles), hot springs (thermoacidophiles) and marshy areas (methanogens). They differ from other bacteria in having a different cell wall structure, responsible for survival in extreme conditions.' },
    { id: 'ar2', topic: '2.1.1 Archaebacteria', t: 'Methanogens are present in the gut of ruminant animals such as cows and buffaloes and are responsible for production of methane (biogas) from their dung.' },
    { id: 'eu1', topic: '2.1.2 Eubacteria', t: 'Eubacteria or \'true bacteria\' are characterised by a rigid cell wall and, if motile, a flagellum.' },
    { id: 'eu2', topic: 'Cyanobacteria', t: 'Cyanobacteria (blue-green algae) have chlorophyll a similar to green plants and are photosynthetic autotrophs. They are unicellular, colonial or filamentous, freshwater/marine or terrestrial; colonies are surrounded by gelatinous sheath and often form blooms in polluted water bodies.' },
    { id: 'eu3', topic: 'Cyanobacteria', t: 'Some cyanobacteria fix atmospheric nitrogen in specialised cells called heterocysts, e.g. Nostoc and Anabaena (Fig 2.2: Nostoc with heterocyst and mucilagenous sheath).' },
    { id: 'eu4', topic: 'Chemosynthetic bacteria', t: 'Chemosynthetic autotrophic bacteria oxidise inorganic substances such as nitrates, nitrites and ammonia and use the released energy for ATP production. They play a great role in recycling nutrients like nitrogen, phosphorous, iron and sulphur.' },
    { id: 'eu5', topic: 'Heterotrophic bacteria', t: 'Heterotrophic bacteria are most abundant in nature; the majority are important decomposers. They help in making curd from milk, production of antibiotics, and fixing nitrogen in legume roots.' },
    { id: 'eu6', topic: 'Heterotrophic bacteria', t: 'Some are pathogens damaging humans, crops, farm animals and pets. Cholera, typhoid, tetanus and citrus canker are well known bacterial diseases.' },
    { id: 'eu7', topic: 'Bacterial reproduction', t: 'Bacteria reproduce mainly by fission (Fig 2.3). Under unfavourable conditions they produce spores. They also show a sort of sexual reproduction by a primitive type of DNA transfer from one bacterium to the other.' },
    { id: 'my1', topic: 'Mycoplasma', t: 'Mycoplasma completely lack a cell wall. They are the smallest living cells known and can survive without oxygen. Many are pathogenic in animals and plants.' },
    { id: 'pr1', topic: '2.2 Kingdom Protista', t: 'All single-celled eukaryotes are placed under Protista, but its boundaries are not well defined. It includes Chrysophytes, Dinoflagellates, Euglenoids, Slime moulds and Protozoans. Members are primarily aquatic.' },
    { id: 'pr2', topic: '2.2 Kingdom Protista', t: 'Protista forms a link with plants, animals and fungi. Being eukaryotes, the cell has a well defined nucleus and membrane-bound organelles; some have flagella or cilia. They reproduce asexually and sexually by cell fusion and zygote formation.' },
    { id: 'ch1', topic: '2.2.1 Chrysophytes', t: 'Chrysophytes include diatoms and golden algae (desmids). Found in fresh water and marine environments; microscopic, float passively in water currents (plankton); most are photosynthetic.' },
    { id: 'ch2', topic: '2.2.1 Chrysophytes', t: 'In diatoms the cell walls form two thin overlapping shells that fit together as in a soap box. The walls are embedded with silica and are indestructible.' },
    { id: 'ch3', topic: '2.2.1 Chrysophytes', t: 'Accumulation of diatom cell wall deposits over billions of years is \'diatomaceous earth\'; being gritty it is used in polishing and filtration of oils and syrups. Diatoms are the chief \'producers\' in the oceans.' },
    { id: 'dn1', topic: '2.2.2 Dinoflagellates', t: 'Dinoflagellates are mostly marine and photosynthetic; appear yellow, green, brown, blue or red depending on main pigments. Cell wall has stiff cellulose plates on the outer surface.' },
    { id: 'dn2', topic: '2.2.2 Dinoflagellates', t: 'Most have two flagella: one lies longitudinally and the other transversely in a furrow between the wall plates.' },
    { id: 'dn3', topic: '2.2.2 Dinoflagellates', t: 'Red dinoflagellates (e.g. Gonyaulax) multiply so rapidly that the sea appears red (red tides). Toxins released may even kill other marine animals such as fishes.' },
    { id: 'eg1', topic: '2.2.3 Euglenoids', t: 'Majority of euglenoids are fresh water organisms found in stagnant water. Instead of a cell wall they have a protein rich layer called pellicle which makes the body flexible. They have two flagella, a short and a long one.' },
    { id: 'eg2', topic: '2.2.3 Euglenoids', t: 'Euglenoids are photosynthetic in sunlight; when deprived of sunlight they behave like heterotrophs by predating on smaller organisms. Their pigments are identical to those in higher plants. Example: Euglena.' },
    { id: 'sm1', topic: '2.2.4 Slime moulds', t: 'Slime moulds are saprophytic protists. The body moves along decaying twigs and leaves engulfing organic material. Under suitable conditions they form an aggregation called plasmodium which may grow and spread over several feet.' },
    { id: 'sm2', topic: '2.2.4 Slime moulds', t: 'During unfavourable conditions the plasmodium differentiates and forms fruiting bodies bearing spores at their tips. Spores possess true walls, are extremely resistant, survive many years and are dispersed by air currents.' },
    { id: 'pz1', topic: '2.2.5 Protozoans', t: 'All protozoans are heterotrophs and live as predators or parasites. They are believed to be primitive relatives of animals. There are four major groups.' },
    { id: 'pz2', topic: 'Amoeboid protozoans', t: 'Amoeboid protozoans live in fresh water, sea water or moist soil; they capture prey by pseudopodia (false feet), e.g. Amoeba. Marine forms have silica shells. Some like Entamoeba are parasites.' },
    { id: 'pz3', topic: 'Flagellated protozoans', t: 'Flagellated protozoans are free-living or parasitic and have flagella. Parasitic forms cause diseases such as sleeping sickness, e.g. Trypanosoma.' },
    { id: 'pz4', topic: 'Ciliated protozoans', t: 'Ciliated protozoans are aquatic, actively moving due to thousands of cilia. A cavity (gullet) opens to the outside; coordinated movement of cilia steers water laden with food into the gullet, e.g. Paramoecium.' },
    { id: 'pz5', topic: 'Sporozoans', t: 'Sporozoans have an infectious spore-like stage in their life cycle. The most notorious is Plasmodium (malarial parasite), which causes malaria.' },
    { id: 'fu1', topic: '2.3 Kingdom Fungi', t: 'Fungi are a unique kingdom of heterotrophic organisms with great diversity. Examples: fungi on moist bread and rotten fruits, mushroom, toadstools; white spots on mustard leaves are due to a parasitic fungus.' },
    { id: 'fu2', topic: '2.3 Kingdom Fungi', t: 'Unicellular yeast is used to make bread and beer. Puccinia causes wheat rust. Penicillium is a source of antibiotics. Fungi are cosmopolitan, prefer warm and humid places — food is kept in the refrigerator to prevent bacterial or fungal spoilage.' },
    { id: 'fu3', topic: 'Structure of fungi', t: 'Except unicellular yeasts, fungi are filamentous; the body consists of long slender thread-like hyphae; the network of hyphae is mycelium. Continuous tubes filled with multinucleated cytoplasm are coenocytic hyphae; others have septae or cross walls.' },
    { id: 'fu4', topic: 'Structure of fungi', t: 'Cell walls of fungi are composed of chitin and polysaccharides.' },
    { id: 'fu5', topic: 'Nutrition of fungi', t: 'Most fungi absorb soluble organic matter from dead substrates — saprophytes. Those depending on living plants and animals are parasites. They also live as symbionts — with algae as lichens and with roots of higher plants as mycorrhiza.' },
    { id: 'fu6', topic: 'Reproduction in fungi', t: 'Vegetative reproduction: fragmentation, fission and budding. Asexual reproduction by spores called conidia, sporangiospores or zoospores. Sexual reproduction by oospores, ascospores and basidiospores, produced in fruiting bodies.' },
    { id: 'fu7', topic: 'Sexual cycle', t: 'Sexual cycle steps: (i) plasmogamy — fusion of protoplasms of two motile or non-motile gametes; (ii) karyogamy — fusion of two nuclei; (iii) meiosis in zygote resulting in haploid spores.' },
    { id: 'fu8', topic: 'Dikaryon', t: 'In some fungi fusion of two haploid cells immediately gives diploid cells (2n). In ascomycetes and basidiomycetes an intervening dikaryotic stage (n + n, two nuclei per cell) occurs — called dikaryon, and the phase dikaryophase. Later nuclei fuse; fruiting bodies form where reduction division gives haploid spores.' },
    { id: 'fu9', topic: 'Basis of classes', t: 'The morphology of mycelium, mode of spore formation and fruiting bodies form the basis for dividing the kingdom into classes.' },
    { id: 'pc1', topic: '2.3.1 Phycomycetes', t: 'Phycomycetes are found in aquatic habitats, on decaying wood in moist damp places, or as obligate parasites on plants. Mycelium is aseptate and coenocytic.' },
    { id: 'pc2', topic: '2.3.1 Phycomycetes', t: 'Asexual reproduction by zoospores (motile) or aplanospores (non-motile), produced endogenously in sporangium. A zygospore forms by fusion of two gametes — similar (isogamous) or dissimilar (anisogamous or oogamous).' },
    { id: 'pc3', topic: '2.3.1 Phycomycetes', t: 'Examples: Mucor, Rhizopus (bread mould) and Albugo (parasitic fungus on mustard).' },
    { id: 'as1', topic: '2.3.2 Ascomycetes', t: 'Ascomycetes (sac-fungi) are mostly multicellular (e.g. Penicillium) or rarely unicellular (yeast — Saccharomyces). They are saprophytic, decomposers, parasitic or coprophilous (growing on dung). Mycelium is branched and septate.' },
    { id: 'as2', topic: '2.3.2 Ascomycetes', t: 'Asexual spores are conidia, produced exogenously on special mycelium called conidiophores. Sexual spores are ascospores produced endogenously in sac-like asci, arranged in fruiting bodies called ascocarps.' },
    { id: 'as3', topic: '2.3.2 Ascomycetes', t: 'Examples: Aspergillus, Claviceps and Neurospora. Neurospora is used extensively in biochemical and genetic work. Morels and truffles are edible delicacies.' },
    { id: 'bs1', topic: '2.3.3 Basidiomycetes', t: 'Basidiomycetes: mushrooms, bracket fungi, puffballs. Grow in soil, on logs and tree stumps, and as parasites in living plants (rusts and smuts). Mycelium is branched and septate.' },
    { id: 'bs2', topic: '2.3.3 Basidiomycetes', t: 'Asexual spores are generally not found; vegetative reproduction by fragmentation is common. Sex organs are absent; plasmogamy is by fusion of two vegetative or somatic cells of different strains or genotypes. The resultant structure is dikaryotic and gives rise to basidium.' },
    { id: 'bs3', topic: '2.3.3 Basidiomycetes', t: 'Karyogamy and meiosis take place in the basidium producing four basidiospores, produced exogenously. Basidia are arranged in fruiting bodies called basidiocarps. Examples: Agaricus (mushroom), Ustilago (smut), Puccinia (rust fungus).' },
    { id: 'dt1', topic: '2.3.4 Deuteromycetes', t: 'Deuteromycetes are known as imperfect fungi because only asexual or vegetative phases are known. When perfect (sexual) stages were discovered they were moved to ascomycetes and basidiomycetes.' },
    { id: 'dt2', topic: '2.3.4 Deuteromycetes', t: 'They reproduce only by asexual spores called conidia. Mycelium is septate and branched. Some are saprophytes or parasites; a large number are decomposers of litter and help in mineral cycling. Examples: Alternaria, Colletotrichum and Trichoderma.' },
    { id: 'pl1', topic: '2.4 Kingdom Plantae', t: 'Kingdom Plantae includes all eukaryotic chlorophyll-containing organisms (plants). A few are partially heterotrophic — insectivorous plants (Bladderwort, Venus fly trap) or parasites (Cuscuta).' },
    { id: 'pl2', topic: '2.4 Kingdom Plantae', t: 'Plant cells are eukaryotic with prominent chloroplasts and cell wall mainly of cellulose. Plantae includes algae, bryophytes, pteridophytes, gymnosperms and angiosperms.' },
    { id: 'pl3', topic: 'Alternation of generations', t: 'Plant life cycle has two phases — diploid sporophytic and haploid gametophytic — that alternate with each other; their lengths and free-living/dependent nature vary among groups. This is alternation of generations.' },
    { id: 'an1', topic: '2.5 Kingdom Animalia', t: 'Animalia: heterotrophic eukaryotic multicellular organisms whose cells lack cell walls. They depend directly or indirectly on plants for food, digest food in an internal cavity and store food reserves as glycogen or fat.' },
    { id: 'an2', topic: '2.5 Kingdom Animalia', t: 'Mode of nutrition is holozoic (ingestion of food). They follow a definite growth pattern, have elaborate sensory and neuromotor mechanism; most are capable of locomotion. Sexual reproduction is by copulation of male and female followed by embryological development.' },
    { id: 'vr1', topic: '2.6 Viruses', t: 'Whittaker\'s five kingdom classification does not mention lichens and acellular organisms like viruses, viroids and prions. Viruses are not considered truly living; they are non-cellular with an inert crystalline structure outside the living cell.' },
    { id: 'vr2', topic: '2.6 Viruses', t: 'Once they infect a cell, viruses take over the machinery of the host cell to replicate themselves, killing the host. Virus means venom or poisonous fluid.' },
    { id: 'vr3', topic: 'History of viruses', t: 'Dmitri Ivanowsky (1892) recognised microbes causing mosaic disease of tobacco, smaller than bacteria as they passed through bacteria-proof filters. M.W. Beijerinck (1898) named the pathogen "virus" and the fluid Contagium vivum fluidum (infectious living fluid).' },
    { id: 'vr4', topic: 'History of viruses', t: 'W.M. Stanley (1935) showed viruses could be crystallised and crystals consist largely of proteins. Viruses are inert outside their specific host cell; they are obligate parasites.' },
    { id: 'vr5', topic: 'Genetic material', t: 'Viruses contain genetic material that is either RNA or DNA — no virus contains both. A virus is a nucleoprotein and the genetic material is infectious.' },
    { id: 'vr6', topic: 'Genetic material', t: 'Plant viruses have single stranded RNA; animal viruses have single or double stranded RNA or double stranded DNA. Bacteriophages (viruses infecting bacteria) are usually double stranded DNA viruses.' },
    { id: 'vr7', topic: 'Structure of virus', t: 'The protein coat called capsid, made of small subunits called capsomeres, protects the nucleic acid; capsomeres are arranged in helical or polyhedral forms. Fig 2.6: TMV (RNA, capsid); bacteriophage (head, collar, sheath, tail fibres).' },
    { id: 'vr8', topic: 'Viral diseases', t: 'Viruses cause mumps, small pox, herpes, influenza and AIDS in humans. In plants symptoms are mosaic formation, leaf rolling and curling, yellowing and vein clearing, dwarfing and stunted growth.' },
    { id: 'vd1', topic: 'Viroids', t: 'In 1971 T.O. Diener discovered viroids — smaller than viruses, causing potato spindle tuber disease. A viroid is free RNA lacking the protein coat; its RNA is of low molecular weight.' },
    { id: 'pn1', topic: 'Prions', t: 'Prions are infectious agents consisting of abnormally folded protein, similar in size to viruses, causing neurological diseases: bovine spongiform encephalopathy (BSE, mad cow disease) in cattle and Creutzfeldt–Jacob disease (CJD) in humans.' },
    { id: 'lc1', topic: 'Lichens', t: 'Lichens are symbiotic (mutually useful) associations between algae and fungi. The algal component is phycobiont (autotrophic) and the fungal component is mycobiont (heterotrophic).' },
    { id: 'lc2', topic: 'Lichens', t: 'Algae prepare food for fungi; fungi provide shelter and absorb mineral nutrients and water for the partner. The association is so close that a lichen looks like a single organism.' },
    { id: 'lc3', topic: 'Lichens', t: 'Lichens are very good pollution indicators — they do not grow in polluted areas.' }
  ],
  qs: [
    { id: 'q1', p: ['in1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Who was the earliest to attempt a more scientific basis for classification?', o: ['Linnaeus', 'Aristotle', 'Whittaker', 'Darwin'], a: 1, e: 'Aristotle used simple morphological characters to classify plants and animals.' },
    { id: 'q2', p: ['in1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Aristotle classified animals on the basis of:', o: ['Presence or absence of red blood', 'Presence of notochord', 'Mode of nutrition', 'Number of legs'], a: 0, e: 'He divided animals into those which had red blood and those that did not.' },
    { id: 'q3', p: ['in1'], type: 'st', lvl: 1, st: ['Aristotle classified plants into trees, shrubs and herbs.', 'Aristotle used chemical constituents for classification.'], a: 2, e: 'He used simple morphological characters.' },
    { id: 'q4', p: ['in2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The Two Kingdom system of classification was developed in the time of:', o: ['Aristotle', 'Linnaeus', 'Whittaker', 'Bentham and Hooker'], a: 1, e: 'In Linnaeus\' time a Two Kingdom system with Plantae and Animalia was developed.' },
    { id: 'q5', p: ['in2'], type: 'stmts', lvl: 2, q: 'Which distinctions were NOT made by the Two Kingdom system? A. Eukaryotes vs prokaryotes B. Unicellular vs multicellular C. Photosynthetic vs non-photosynthetic D. Plants vs animals', o: ['A and B only', 'A, B and C', 'D only', 'A, B, C and D'], a: 1, e: 'It did separate plants from animals, but not A, B or C.' },
    { id: 'q6', p: ['in3'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'The two kingdom classification was adequate to classify all organisms.', R: 'A large number of organisms did not fall into either category.' }, a: 3, e: 'It was found inadequate, so A is false; R is true.' },
    { id: 'q7', p: ['in3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Besides gross morphology, which characters were felt necessary for classification?', o: ['Only colour', 'Cell structure, nature of wall, mode of nutrition, habitat, reproduction, evolutionary relationships', 'Only uses to humans', 'Only size'], a: 1, e: 'These were included as classification systems changed over time.' },
    { id: 'q8', p: ['wh1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The Five Kingdom Classification was proposed by:', o: ['Linnaeus (1758)', 'R.H. Whittaker (1969)', 'Copeland (1956)', 'Haeckel (1866)'], a: 1, e: 'R.H. Whittaker (1969).' },
    { id: 'q9', p: ['wh1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Which was NOT a main criterion used by Whittaker?', o: ['Cell structure', 'Mode of nutrition', 'Phylogenetic relationships', 'Economic importance'], a: 3, e: 'Criteria: cell structure, body organisation, mode of nutrition, reproduction and phylogenetic relationships.' },
    { id: 'q10', p: ['wh2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'The three-domain system divides which kingdom into two domains?', o: ['Protista', 'Monera', 'Fungi', 'Plantae'], a: 1, e: 'It divides Kingdom Monera into two domains, giving a six kingdom classification.' },
    { id: 'q11', p: ['wh2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['The three-domain system leads to a six kingdom classification.', 'In the three-domain system, all eukaryotic kingdoms are placed in the third domain.'], a: 0, e: 'Both true.' },
    { id: 'q12', p: ['t21a'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Nuclear membrane is absent in members of kingdom:', o: ['Protista', 'Monera', 'Fungi', 'Plantae'], a: 1, e: 'Monera is prokaryotic, nuclear membrane absent.' },
    { id: 'q13', p: ['t21a', 't21c'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Members of Monera have cellular body organisation.', R: 'Members of Monera are eukaryotic.' }, a: 2, e: 'Monera are prokaryotic — R false.' },
    { id: 'q14', p: ['t21b'], type: 'match', lvl: 2, q: 'Match the kingdom with the nature of its cell wall (Table 2.1).', match: { h: ['Kingdom', 'Cell wall'], l: ['Monera', 'Fungi', 'Plantae', 'Animalia'], r: ['Absent', 'Cellulose', 'Present with chitin', 'Non-cellulosic (polysaccharide + amino acid)'] }, o: ['A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I', 'A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I', 'A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I', 'A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV'], a: 0, e: 'Monera non-cellulosic; Fungi chitin; Plantae cellulose; Animalia absent.' },
    { id: 'q15', p: ['t21b'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'In Kingdom Protista, the cell wall is:', o: ['Absent in all', 'Present in some', 'Made of chitin in all', 'Present in all, of cellulose'], a: 1, e: 'Table 2.1: Protista – present in some.' },
    { id: 'q16', p: ['t21c'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Body organisation "multicellular/loose tissue" is characteristic of:', o: ['Monera', 'Protista', 'Fungi', 'Animalia'], a: 2, e: 'Table 2.1: Fungi – multicellular/loose tissue.' },
    { id: 'q17', p: ['t21c'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Tissue/organ/organ system level of body organisation is found in:', o: ['Plantae', 'Animalia', 'Fungi', 'Protista'], a: 1, e: 'Animalia – tissue/organ/organ system; Plantae – tissue/organ.' },
    { id: 'q18', p: ['t21d'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Which kingdom includes both chemosynthetic and photosynthetic autotrophs as well as heterotrophs?', o: ['Monera', 'Fungi', 'Plantae', 'Animalia'], a: 0, e: 'Monera: autotrophic (chemosynthetic and photosynthetic) and heterotrophic.' },
    { id: 'q19', p: ['t21d'], type: 'match', lvl: 2, q: 'Match the kingdom with its mode of nutrition.', match: { h: ['Kingdom', 'Nutrition'], l: ['Fungi', 'Plantae', 'Animalia', 'Protista'], r: ['Photosynthetic autotrophic and heterotrophic', 'Holozoic/saprophytic etc.', 'Photosynthetic autotrophic', 'Saprophytic/parasitic heterotrophic'] }, o: ['A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I', 'A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I', 'A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III', 'A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II'], a: 0, e: 'Fungi heterotrophic (saprophytic/parasitic); Plantae photosynthetic; Animalia holozoic etc.; Protista both.' },
    { id: 'q20', p: ['old1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'In earlier classification, what single character unified all organisms included under \'Plants\'?', o: ['Chlorophyll', 'A cell wall', 'Multicellularity', 'Eukaryotic nucleus'], a: 1, e: 'The character that unified this whole kingdom was that all had a cell wall.' },
    { id: 'q21', p: ['old1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Earlier classification placed Chlamydomonas and Spirogyra together under algae.', R: 'It did not separate unicellular and multicellular organisms.' }, a: 0, e: 'It grouped unicellular with multicellular ones.' },
    { id: 'q22', p: ['old2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Fungi were separated into Kingdom Fungi mainly because:', o: ['They have cellulosic walls', 'They are heterotrophic and have chitin in their walls', 'They are prokaryotic', 'They are unicellular'], a: 1, e: 'Fungi are heterotrophic with chitin walls; green plants autotrophic with cellulose walls.' },
    { id: 'q23', p: ['old2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Green plants have chitin in their cell walls.', 'Fungi have chitin in their cell walls.'], a: 3, e: 'Green plants have a cellulosic cell wall.' },
    { id: 'q24', p: ['old3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Kingdom Protista brought together Chlamydomonas and Chlorella with:', o: ['Bacteria and cyanobacteria', 'Paramoecium and Amoeba', 'Mushrooms and yeasts', 'Mosses and ferns'], a: 1, e: 'Chlamydomonas, Chlorella (with cell walls) and Paramoecium, Amoeba (without cell walls).' },
    { id: 'q25', p: ['old3'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Organisms placed in different kingdoms earlier were put together in Protista.', R: 'The criteria for classification changed.' }, a: 0, e: 'NCERT: this happened because the criteria for classification changed.' },
    { id: 'q26', p: ['old3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'All prokaryotic organisms are grouped under kingdom:', o: ['Protista', 'Monera', 'Fungi', 'Plantae'], a: 1, e: 'All prokaryotes are in Kingdom Monera.' },
    { id: 'q27', p: ['old4'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'A phylogenetic classification system is based on:', o: ['Economic uses', 'Evolutionary relationships', 'Habitat only', 'Size'], a: 1, e: 'Phylogenetic, i.e., based on evolutionary relationships.' },
    { id: 'q28', p: ['old4'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Changes in classification will take place in future too.', 'The aim is a system reflecting morphological, physiological, reproductive similarities and evolutionary relationships.'], a: 0, e: 'Both are true.' },
    { id: 'q29', p: ['mo1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The sole members of Kingdom Monera are:', o: ['Protozoans', 'Bacteria', 'Fungi', 'Algae'], a: 1, e: 'Bacteria are the sole members of Kingdom Monera.' },
    { id: 'q30', p: ['mo1'], type: 'st', lvl: 1, st: ['Bacteria are the most abundant micro-organisms.', 'Bacteria cannot survive in extreme habitats like hot springs and deep oceans.'], a: 2, e: 'They live in extreme habitats where few other life forms survive.' },
    { id: 'q31', p: ['mo2'], type: 'match', lvl: 1, q: 'Match the bacterial shape with its name.', match: { h: ['Shape', 'Name'], l: ['Spherical', 'Rod-shaped', 'Comma-shaped', 'Spiral'], r: ['Spirillum', 'Vibrium', 'Bacillus', 'Coccus'] }, o: ['A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I', 'A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II', 'A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I', 'A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV'], a: 0, e: 'Coccus spherical; Bacillus rod; Vibrium comma; Spirillum spiral.' },
    { id: 'q32', p: ['mo2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Comma-shaped bacteria are called:', o: ['Cocci', 'Bacilli', 'Vibrio', 'Spirilla'], a: 2, e: 'The comma-shaped Vibrium (pl. vibrio).' },
    { id: 'q33', p: ['mo3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'As a group, which organisms show the most extensive metabolic diversity?', o: ['Fungi', 'Bacteria', 'Protozoans', 'Algae'], a: 1, e: 'Bacteria show the most extensive metabolic diversity.' },
    { id: 'q34', p: ['mo3'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'The vast majority of bacteria are heterotrophs.', R: 'Bacteria have very complex structure.' }, a: 2, e: 'Bacterial structure is very simple, though behaviour is complex — R false.' },
    { id: 'q35', p: ['ar1'], type: 'match', lvl: 2, q: 'Match the archaebacteria with their habitat.', match: { h: ['Group', 'Habitat'], l: ['Halophiles', 'Thermoacidophiles', 'Methanogens'], r: ['Marshy areas', 'Hot springs', 'Extreme salty areas'] }, o: ['A-III, B-II, C-I', 'A-II, B-III, C-I', 'A-I, B-II, C-III', 'A-III, B-I, C-II'], a: 0, e: 'Halophiles – salty; thermoacidophiles – hot springs; methanogens – marshy areas.' },
    { id: 'q36', p: ['ar1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Archaebacteria cannot survive in extreme conditions.', R: 'They have a different cell wall structure from other bacteria.' }, a: 3, e: 'They live in the harshest habitats, so A is false; R is true.' },
    { id: 'q37', p: ['ar2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Methanogens present in the gut of ruminants are responsible for:', o: ['Nitrogen fixation', 'Production of methane (biogas) from dung', 'Production of antibiotics', 'Curd formation'], a: 1, e: 'They produce methane (biogas) from the dung of cows and buffaloes.' },
    { id: 'q38', p: ['ar2', 'ar1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Methanogens belong to:', o: ['Eubacteria', 'Archaebacteria', 'Cyanobacteria', 'Mycoplasma'], a: 1, e: 'Methanogens are archaebacteria.' },
    { id: 'q39', p: ['eu1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Eubacteria are characterised by:', o: ['Absence of cell wall', 'A rigid cell wall and, if motile, a flagellum', 'Pellicle', 'Silica shells'], a: 1, e: 'True bacteria have a rigid cell wall and, if motile, a flagellum.' },
    { id: 'q40', p: ['eu1', 'my1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Mycoplasma have a rigid cell wall.', 'Eubacteria are called \'true bacteria\'.'], a: 3, e: 'Mycoplasma completely lack a cell wall.' },
    { id: 'q41', p: ['eu2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Cyanobacteria have chlorophyll a similar to green plants and are:', o: ['Chemosynthetic autotrophs', 'Photosynthetic autotrophs', 'Saprophytes', 'Parasites'], a: 1, e: 'Cyanobacteria are photosynthetic autotrophs.' },
    { id: 'q42', p: ['eu2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Cyanobacteria often form blooms in:', o: ['Clean mountain streams', 'Polluted water bodies', 'Deserts', 'Deep oceans only'], a: 1, e: 'They often form blooms in polluted water bodies.' },
    { id: 'q43', p: ['eu2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Colonies of cyanobacteria are generally surrounded by a gelatinous sheath.', 'Cyanobacteria are always unicellular.'], a: 2, e: 'They are unicellular, colonial or filamentous.' },
    { id: 'q44', p: ['eu3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Specialised cells of cyanobacteria that fix atmospheric nitrogen are:', o: ['Akinetes', 'Heterocysts', 'Gemmae', 'Conidia'], a: 1, e: 'Nitrogen is fixed in heterocysts, e.g., Nostoc and Anabaena.' },
    { id: 'q45', p: ['eu3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Which is a nitrogen fixing cyanobacterium with heterocysts?', o: ['Nostoc', 'Euglena', 'Gonyaulax', 'Mucor'], a: 0, e: 'Nostoc and Anabaena.' },
    { id: 'q46', p: ['eu4'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Chemosynthetic autotrophic bacteria obtain energy by:', o: ['Using sunlight', 'Oxidising inorganic substances like nitrates, nitrites and ammonia', 'Ingesting food', 'Parasitism'], a: 1, e: 'They oxidise inorganic substances and use released energy for ATP production.' },
    { id: 'q47', p: ['eu4'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Chemosynthetic bacteria have no role in nutrient recycling.', R: 'They help recycle nitrogen, phosphorous, iron and sulphur.' }, a: 3, e: 'They play a great role in recycling nutrients, so A is false; R is true.' },
    { id: 'q48', p: ['eu5'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The most abundant bacteria in nature are:', o: ['Chemosynthetic autotrophs', 'Photosynthetic autotrophs', 'Heterotrophic bacteria', 'Archaebacteria'], a: 2, e: 'Heterotrophic bacteria are most abundant; majority are important decomposers.' },
    { id: 'q49', p: ['eu5'], type: 'stmts', lvl: 2, q: 'Heterotrophic bacteria are helpful in: A. making curd from milk B. production of antibiotics C. fixing nitrogen in legume roots D. causing citrus canker', o: ['A and B only', 'A, B and C', 'B, C and D', 'A, B, C and D'], a: 1, e: 'Citrus canker is a disease, not a helpful role.' },
    { id: 'q50', p: ['eu6'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Which of the following is NOT a bacterial disease?', o: ['Cholera', 'Typhoid', 'Citrus canker', 'Influenza'], a: 3, e: 'Influenza is viral; cholera, typhoid, tetanus and citrus canker are bacterial.' },
    { id: 'q51', p: ['eu6'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Citrus canker is caused by:', o: ['Virus', 'Bacteria', 'Fungus', 'Viroid'], a: 1, e: 'Citrus canker is a well known bacterial disease.' },
    { id: 'q52', p: ['eu7'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Bacteria reproduce mainly by:', o: ['Budding', 'Fission', 'Conidia', 'Fragmentation'], a: 1, e: 'Bacteria reproduce mainly by fission.' },
    { id: 'q53', p: ['eu7'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Bacteria produce spores under unfavourable conditions.', 'Bacteria show a sort of sexual reproduction by a primitive type of DNA transfer.'], a: 0, e: 'Both true.' },
    { id: 'q54', p: ['my1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The smallest living cells known, which completely lack a cell wall, are:', o: ['Viruses', 'Mycoplasma', 'Viroids', 'Diatoms'], a: 1, e: 'Mycoplasma.' },
    { id: 'q55', p: ['my1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Mycoplasma can survive without oxygen.', R: 'Mycoplasma are pathogenic in animals and plants.' }, a: 1, e: 'Both true, but pathogenicity does not explain anaerobic survival.' },
    { id: 'q56', p: ['pr1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'All single-celled eukaryotes are placed under kingdom:', o: ['Monera', 'Protista', 'Fungi', 'Plantae'], a: 1, e: 'Protista.' },
    { id: 'q57', p: ['pr1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Which group is NOT included in Protista in NCERT?', o: ['Chrysophytes', 'Euglenoids', 'Slime moulds', 'Cyanobacteria'], a: 3, e: 'Cyanobacteria are in Monera.' },
    { id: 'q58', p: ['pr2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'A protistan cell contains a well defined nucleus.', R: 'Protists are eukaryotes.' }, a: 0, e: 'Being eukaryotes, protists have a nucleus and membrane-bound organelles.' },
    { id: 'q59', p: ['pr2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Protists reproduce sexually by a process involving:', o: ['Conjugation tube only', 'Cell fusion and zygote formation', 'Basidiospores', 'Double fertilisation'], a: 1, e: 'Sexually by cell fusion and zygote formation.' },
    { id: 'q60', p: ['ch1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Chrysophytes include:', o: ['Diatoms and desmids', 'Euglena and Trypanosoma', 'Mucor and Rhizopus', 'Nostoc and Anabaena'], a: 0, e: 'Diatoms and golden algae (desmids).' },
    { id: 'q61', p: ['ch1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Microscopic organisms that float passively in water currents are called:', o: ['Nekton', 'Plankton', 'Benthos', 'Periphyton'], a: 1, e: 'Chrysophytes float passively in water currents (plankton).' },
    { id: 'q62', p: ['ch2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The cell walls of diatoms are embedded with:', o: ['Calcium', 'Silica', 'Chitin', 'Pectin'], a: 1, e: 'Walls are embedded with silica and are indestructible.' },
    { id: 'q63', p: ['ch2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Diatom cell walls fit together like a:', o: ['Soap box', 'Lock and key', 'Spiral', 'Cup'], a: 0, e: 'Two thin overlapping shells fit together as in a soap box.' },
    { id: 'q64', p: ['ch3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Diatomaceous earth is used in:', o: ['Making bread', 'Polishing and filtration of oils and syrups', 'Producing antibiotics', 'Biogas production'], a: 1, e: 'Being gritty it is used in polishing and filtration of oils and syrups.' },
    { id: 'q65', p: ['ch3', 'ch2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Diatoms have left behind large amounts of cell wall deposits in their habitat.', R: 'Diatom walls are embedded with silica and are indestructible.' }, a: 0, e: 'Indestructible walls accumulate as diatomaceous earth.' },
    { id: 'q66', p: ['ch3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The chief \'producers\' in the oceans are:', o: ['Dinoflagellates', 'Diatoms', 'Euglenoids', 'Red algae'], a: 1, e: 'Diatoms are the chief producers in the oceans.' },
    { id: 'q67', p: ['dn1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'In dinoflagellates, the cell wall has:', o: ['Silica shells', 'Stiff cellulose plates on the outer surface', 'A pellicle', 'Chitin'], a: 1, e: 'Cell wall has stiff cellulose plates on the outer surface.' },
    { id: 'q68', p: ['dn1'], type: 'st', lvl: 1, st: ['Dinoflagellates always appear green.', 'Dinoflagellates are mostly marine and photosynthetic.'], a: 3, e: 'They appear yellow, green, brown, blue or red depending on pigments.' },
    { id: 'q69', p: ['dn2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'In dinoflagellates, the two flagella are arranged as:', o: ['Both apical', 'One longitudinal and one transverse in a furrow between wall plates', 'Both lateral and equal', 'One short and one long, both anterior'], a: 1, e: 'One lies longitudinally, the other transversely in a furrow.' },
    { id: 'q173', p: ['dn2', 'dn1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Most dinoflagellates have a single flagellum.', 'The cell wall of dinoflagellates has stiff cellulose plates on the outer surface.'], a: 3, e: 'Most have two flagella — one longitudinal, one transverse in a furrow.' },
    { id: 'q70', p: ['dn3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Red tides are caused by rapid multiplication of:', o: ['Gonyaulax', 'Euglena', 'Diatoms', 'Nostoc'], a: 0, e: 'Red dinoflagellates like Gonyaulax make the sea appear red.' },
    { id: 'q71', p: ['dn3'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Red tides are harmless to fishes.', R: 'Toxins released by large numbers of red dinoflagellates may kill marine animals.' }, a: 3, e: 'Toxins released may kill fishes, so A is false; R is true.' },
    { id: 'q72', p: ['eg1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Instead of a cell wall, euglenoids have:', o: ['A protein rich pellicle', 'A silica shell', 'Cellulose plates', 'Chitin'], a: 0, e: 'A protein rich layer called pellicle makes their body flexible.' },
    { id: 'q73', p: ['eg1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Euglenoids have:', o: ['Two equal flagella', 'Two flagella — a short and a long one', 'Thousands of cilia', 'No flagella'], a: 1, e: 'Two flagella, a short and a long one.' },
    { id: 'q74', p: ['eg2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Euglena behaves like a heterotroph when deprived of sunlight.', R: 'It predates on other smaller organisms in the absence of light.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A — mixotrophic nature.' },
    { id: 'q75', p: ['eg2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Pigments of euglenoids are identical to those present in higher plants.', 'Euglenoids are mostly marine.'], a: 2, e: 'Majority are fresh water organisms in stagnant water.' },
    { id: 'q76', p: ['sm1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Slime moulds are:', o: ['Parasitic protists', 'Saprophytic protists', 'Photosynthetic protists', 'Prokaryotes'], a: 1, e: 'Slime moulds are saprophytic protists.' },
    { id: 'q77', p: ['sm1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Under suitable conditions, slime moulds form an aggregation called:', o: ['Mycelium', 'Plasmodium', 'Thallus', 'Colony'], a: 1, e: 'An aggregation called plasmodium which may spread over several feet.' },
    { id: 'q78', p: ['sm2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Spores of slime moulds are dispersed by:', o: ['Water', 'Air currents', 'Insects', 'Animals'], a: 1, e: 'The spores are dispersed by air currents.' },
    { id: 'q79', p: ['sm2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Spores of slime moulds survive for many years even under adverse conditions.', R: 'The spores possess true walls and are extremely resistant.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q80', p: ['pz1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Protozoans are believed to be primitive relatives of:', o: ['Plants', 'Animals', 'Fungi', 'Bacteria'], a: 1, e: 'They are believed to be primitive relatives of animals.' },
    { id: 'q81', p: ['pz1'], type: 'st', lvl: 1, st: ['All protozoans are heterotrophs.', 'There are four major groups of protozoans.'], a: 0, e: 'Both true.' },
    { id: 'q82', p: ['pz2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Amoeboid protozoans capture prey by:', o: ['Cilia', 'Flagella', 'Pseudopodia', 'Gullet'], a: 2, e: 'They put out pseudopodia (false feet).' },
    { id: 'q83', p: ['pz2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'A parasitic amoeboid protozoan is:', o: ['Entamoeba', 'Paramoecium', 'Trypanosoma', 'Euglena'], a: 0, e: 'Some such as Entamoeba are parasites. Marine forms have silica shells.' },
    { id: 'q84', p: ['pz3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Sleeping sickness is caused by:', o: ['Plasmodium', 'Trypanosoma', 'Entamoeba', 'Paramoecium'], a: 1, e: 'Trypanosoma, a flagellated protozoan.' },
    { id: 'q85', p: ['pz3', 'pz4'], type: 'match', lvl: 2, q: 'Match the protozoan group with its example.', match: { h: ['Group', 'Example'], l: ['Amoeboid', 'Flagellated', 'Ciliated', 'Sporozoan'], r: ['Plasmodium', 'Paramoecium', 'Trypanosoma', 'Amoeba'] }, o: ['A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I', 'A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II', 'A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I', 'A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV'], a: 0, e: 'Amoeba; Trypanosoma; Paramoecium; Plasmodium.' },
    { id: 'q86', p: ['pz4'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'In Paramoecium, water laden with food is steered into the gullet by:', o: ['Pseudopodia', 'Coordinated movement of rows of cilia', 'Flagellum', 'Contractile vacuole'], a: 1, e: 'Coordinated movement of rows of cilia.' },
    { id: 'q87', p: ['pz5'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The malarial parasite Plasmodium belongs to:', o: ['Amoeboid protozoans', 'Flagellated protozoans', 'Ciliated protozoans', 'Sporozoans'], a: 3, e: 'Sporozoans have an infectious spore-like stage; Plasmodium is the most notorious.' },
    { id: 'q88', p: ['pz5'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Plasmodium is placed under ciliated protozoans.', R: 'It has an infectious spore-like stage in its life cycle.' }, a: 3, e: 'Plasmodium is a sporozoan, so A is false; R is true.' },
    { id: 'q89', p: ['fu1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'White spots seen on mustard leaves are due to:', o: ['A bacterium', 'A parasitic fungus', 'A virus', 'Mineral deficiency'], a: 1, e: 'Due to a parasitic fungus (Albugo).' },
    { id: 'q90', p: ['fu1', 'fu2'], type: 'st', lvl: 1, st: ['Fungi prefer to grow in cold and dry places.', 'Fungi constitute a unique kingdom of heterotrophic organisms.'], a: 3, e: 'They prefer warm and humid places.' },
    { id: 'q91', p: ['fu2'], type: 'match', lvl: 2, q: 'Match the fungus with its importance.', match: { h: ['Fungus', 'Importance'], l: ['Yeast', 'Puccinia', 'Penicillium'], r: ['Source of antibiotics', 'Wheat rust', 'Bread and beer'] }, o: ['A-III, B-II, C-I', 'A-II, B-III, C-I', 'A-I, B-II, C-III', 'A-III, B-I, C-II'], a: 0, e: 'Yeast – bread/beer; Puccinia – wheat rust; Penicillium – antibiotics.' },
    { id: 'q92', p: ['fu2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'We keep food in the refrigerator.', R: 'It prevents food from going bad due to bacterial or fungal infections.' }, a: 0, e: 'Fungi prefer warm, humid places; cold slows spoilage.' },
    { id: 'q93', p: ['fu3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The network of hyphae in fungi is known as:', o: ['Thallus', 'Mycelium', 'Plasmodium', 'Rhizoid'], a: 1, e: 'The network of hyphae is known as mycelium.' },
    { id: 'q94', p: ['fu3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Hyphae that are continuous tubes filled with multinucleated cytoplasm are called:', o: ['Septate hyphae', 'Coenocytic hyphae', 'Dikaryotic hyphae', 'Rhizoids'], a: 1, e: 'They are called coenocytic hyphae.' },
    { id: 'q95', p: ['fu3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Which fungi are NOT filamentous?', o: ['Mushrooms', 'Yeasts', 'Mucor', 'Penicillium'], a: 1, e: 'With the exception of unicellular yeasts, fungi are filamentous.' },
    { id: 'q96', p: ['fu4'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Cell walls of fungi are composed of:', o: ['Cellulose and pectin', 'Chitin and polysaccharides', 'Peptidoglycan', 'Silica'], a: 1, e: 'Chitin and polysaccharides.' },
    { id: 'q97', p: ['fu4', 'old2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Fungal cell walls contain chitin.', 'Fungal cell walls are mainly made of cellulose like green plants.'], a: 2, e: 'Fungi have chitin; plants have cellulose.' },
    { id: 'q98', p: ['fu5'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Fungi that absorb soluble organic matter from dead substrates are called:', o: ['Parasites', 'Saprophytes', 'Symbionts', 'Autotrophs'], a: 1, e: 'Saprophytes.' },
    { id: 'q99', p: ['fu5'], type: 'match', lvl: 2, q: 'Match the symbiotic association with its partners.', match: { h: ['Association', 'Partners'], l: ['Lichen', 'Mycorrhiza'], r: ['Fungi and roots of higher plants', 'Fungi and algae'] }, o: ['A-II, B-I', 'A-I, B-II', 'A-I, B-I', 'A-II, B-II'], a: 0, e: 'Lichen – fungi + algae; mycorrhiza – fungi + roots of higher plants.' },
    { id: 'q100', p: ['fu6'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Which is NOT an asexual spore of fungi?', o: ['Conidia', 'Sporangiospores', 'Zoospores', 'Ascospores'], a: 3, e: 'Ascospores are sexual spores (with oospores and basidiospores).' },
    { id: 'q101', p: ['fu6'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Vegetative reproduction in fungi takes place by:', o: ['Fragmentation, fission and budding', 'Conidia only', 'Zygospores', 'Basidiospores'], a: 0, e: 'Vegetative means – fragmentation, fission and budding.' },
    { id: 'q102', p: ['fu7'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Fusion of protoplasms between two gametes in fungi is called:', o: ['Karyogamy', 'Plasmogamy', 'Meiosis', 'Syngamy of nuclei'], a: 1, e: 'Plasmogamy.' },
    { id: 'q103', p: ['fu7'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'The correct sequence of the sexual cycle in fungi is:', o: ['Karyogamy → plasmogamy → meiosis', 'Plasmogamy → karyogamy → meiosis', 'Meiosis → plasmogamy → karyogamy', 'Plasmogamy → meiosis → karyogamy'], a: 1, e: 'Plasmogamy, then karyogamy, then meiosis in zygote giving haploid spores.' },
    { id: 'q104', p: ['fu8'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'In ascomycetes and basidiomycetes, a cell with two nuclei (n + n) is called:', o: ['Diploid', 'Dikaryon', 'Coenocyte', 'Zygote'], a: 1, e: 'Such a condition is called a dikaryon and the phase is dikaryophase.' },
    { id: 'q105', p: ['fu8'], type: 'ar', lvl: 3, ar: { A: 'In some fungi, fusion of two haploid cells immediately results in diploid cells.', R: 'In ascomycetes and basidiomycetes an intervening dikaryotic stage occurs.' }, a: 1, e: 'Both true but R is about other fungi; it does not explain A.' },
    { id: 'q106', p: ['fu8'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Reduction division in fruiting bodies forms diploid spores.', 'Fungi form fruiting bodies in which reduction division occurs.'], a: 3, e: 'It leads to formation of haploid spores.' },
    { id: 'q107', p: ['fu9'], type: 'stmts', lvl: 2, q: 'Which form the basis for dividing Kingdom Fungi into classes? A. Morphology of mycelium B. Mode of spore formation C. Fruiting bodies D. Colour of fungus', o: ['A, B and C', 'A and B only', 'B and D', 'A, B, C and D'], a: 0, e: 'Morphology of mycelium, mode of spore formation and fruiting bodies.' },
    { id: 'q108', p: ['fu9', 'pc1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Classes of fungi are NOT based on:', o: ['Morphology of mycelium', 'Mode of spore formation', 'Fruiting bodies', 'Mode of nutrition of host'], a: 3, e: 'The three listed bases are mycelium, spore formation and fruiting bodies.' },
    { id: 'q109', p: ['pc1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The mycelium of phycomycetes is:', o: ['Septate and branched', 'Aseptate and coenocytic', 'Dikaryotic', 'Absent'], a: 1, e: 'Mycelium is aseptate and coenocytic.' },
    { id: 'q110', p: ['pc1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Phycomycetes are found:', o: ['Only in deserts', 'In aquatic habitats, decaying wood in moist places, or as obligate parasites on plants', 'Only on dung', 'Only in hot springs'], a: 1, e: 'As per NCERT habitats of phycomycetes.' },
    { id: 'q111', p: ['pc2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'In phycomycetes, a zygospore is formed by:', o: ['Fusion of two gametes', 'Meiosis', 'Budding', 'Conidia'], a: 0, e: 'A zygospore is formed by fusion of two gametes.' },
    { id: 'q112', p: ['pc2'], type: 'match', lvl: 2, q: 'Match the spore with its nature.', match: { h: ['Spore', 'Nature'], l: ['Zoospore', 'Aplanospore'], r: ['Non-motile', 'Motile'] }, o: ['A-II, B-I', 'A-I, B-II', 'A-I, B-I', 'A-II, B-II'], a: 0, e: 'Zoospores motile; aplanospores non-motile; both endogenous in sporangium.' },
    { id: 'q113', p: ['pc3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Albugo, a parasitic fungus on mustard, belongs to:', o: ['Ascomycetes', 'Phycomycetes', 'Basidiomycetes', 'Deuteromycetes'], a: 1, e: 'Mucor, Rhizopus and Albugo are phycomycetes.' },
    { id: 'q114', p: ['pc3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Bread mould Rhizopus is a member of:', o: ['Phycomycetes', 'Ascomycetes', 'Basidiomycetes', 'Deuteromycetes'], a: 0, e: 'Rhizopus is a phycomycete.' },
    { id: 'q115', p: ['as1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Sac-fungi are:', o: ['Phycomycetes', 'Ascomycetes', 'Basidiomycetes', 'Deuteromycetes'], a: 1, e: 'Ascomycetes are commonly known as sac-fungi.' },
    { id: 'q116', p: ['as1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Fungi growing on dung are called:', o: ['Saprophytic', 'Coprophilous', 'Halophilic', 'Epiphytic'], a: 1, e: 'Coprophilous (growing on dung).' },
    { id: 'q117', p: ['as1', 'as2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Yeast (Saccharomyces) is a unicellular ascomycete.', 'Mycelium of ascomycetes is aseptate.'], a: 2, e: 'Mycelium is branched and septate.' },
    { id: 'q118', p: ['as2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'In ascomycetes, conidia are produced exogenously on special mycelium called:', o: ['Sporangiophores', 'Conidiophores', 'Basidia', 'Asci'], a: 1, e: 'Conidiophores.' },
    { id: 'q119', p: ['as2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Ascospores are produced:', o: ['Exogenously on basidium', 'Endogenously in sac-like asci', 'Exogenously on conidiophores', 'In zygospores'], a: 1, e: 'Ascospores are produced endogenously in asci, arranged in ascocarps.' },
    { id: 'q120', p: ['as3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Which fungus is used extensively in biochemical and genetic work?', o: ['Agaricus', 'Neurospora', 'Albugo', 'Ustilago'], a: 1, e: 'Neurospora.' },
    { id: 'q121', p: ['as3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Morels and truffles, considered delicacies, belong to:', o: ['Basidiomycetes', 'Ascomycetes', 'Phycomycetes', 'Deuteromycetes'], a: 1, e: 'Many ascomycetes like morels and truffles are edible.' },
    { id: 'q122', p: ['bs1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Mushrooms, bracket fungi and puffballs belong to:', o: ['Ascomycetes', 'Basidiomycetes', 'Phycomycetes', 'Deuteromycetes'], a: 1, e: 'Commonly known forms of basidiomycetes.' },
    { id: 'q123', p: ['bs1', 'bs3'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Rusts and smuts are basidiomycetes.', R: 'Basidiomycetes grow as parasites in living plant bodies.' }, a: 0, e: 'Rusts and smuts are parasitic basidiomycetes.' },
    { id: 'q124', p: ['bs2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'In basidiomycetes, plasmogamy is brought about by:', o: ['Fusion of gametes formed in sex organs', 'Fusion of two vegetative or somatic cells of different strains', 'Conidia', 'Zoospores'], a: 1, e: 'Sex organs are absent; plasmogamy by fusion of two somatic cells of different strains/genotypes.' },
    { id: 'q125', p: ['bs2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Sex organs are well developed in basidiomycetes.', 'Asexual spores are generally not found in basidiomycetes.'], a: 3, e: 'Sex organs are absent.' },
    { id: 'q126', p: ['bs3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'How many basidiospores are produced in a basidium?', o: ['Two', 'Four', 'Eight', 'Many'], a: 1, e: 'Karyogamy and meiosis in the basidium produce four basidiospores.' },
    { id: 'q127', p: ['bs3'], type: 'match', lvl: 2, q: 'Match the fungus with its common name.', match: { h: ['Fungus', 'Common name'], l: ['Agaricus', 'Ustilago', 'Puccinia'], r: ['Rust fungus', 'Smut', 'Mushroom'] }, o: ['A-III, B-II, C-I', 'A-II, B-III, C-I', 'A-I, B-II, C-III', 'A-III, B-I, C-II'], a: 0, e: 'Agaricus – mushroom; Ustilago – smut; Puccinia – rust.' },
    { id: 'q128', p: ['bs3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Fruiting bodies of basidiomycetes are called:', o: ['Ascocarps', 'Basidiocarps', 'Sporangia', 'Conidiophores'], a: 1, e: 'Basidia are arranged in fruiting bodies called basidiocarps.' },
    { id: 'q129', p: ['dt1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Deuteromycetes are known as imperfect fungi because:', o: ['They are parasites', 'Only their asexual or vegetative phases are known', 'They lack mycelium', 'They are unicellular'], a: 1, e: 'Only the asexual or vegetative phases are known.' },
    { id: 'q130', p: ['dt1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Many members of deuteromycetes were later moved to ascomycetes and basidiomycetes.', R: 'Their perfect (sexual) stages were discovered.' }, a: 0, e: 'Once sexual stages were found, they were moved to the right classes.' },
    { id: 'q131', p: ['dt2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Which is NOT a deuteromycete?', o: ['Alternaria', 'Colletotrichum', 'Trichoderma', 'Agaricus'], a: 3, e: 'Agaricus is a basidiomycete.' },
    { id: 'q132', p: ['dt2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'A large number of deuteromycetes help in:', o: ['Nitrogen fixation', 'Decomposition of litter and mineral cycling', 'Photosynthesis', 'Red tides'], a: 1, e: 'They are decomposers of litter and help in mineral cycling.' },
    { id: 'q133', p: ['dt2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Deuteromycetes reproduce only by asexual spores called conidia.', 'Mycelium of deuteromycetes is aseptate.'], a: 2, e: 'Mycelium is septate and branched.' },
    { id: 'q134', p: ['pl1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Cuscuta is an example of a plant that is:', o: ['Insectivorous', 'Parasitic', 'Saprophytic fungus', 'Autotrophic only'], a: 1, e: 'Cuscuta is a parasite; Bladderwort and Venus fly trap are insectivorous.' },
    { id: 'q135', p: ['pl1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Which of the following are insectivorous plants?', o: ['Bladderwort and Venus fly trap', 'Cuscuta and Bladderwort', 'Funaria and Marchantia', 'Albugo and Puccinia'], a: 0, e: 'Bladderwort and Venus fly trap.' },
    { id: 'q136', p: ['pl2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The cell wall of plant cells is mainly made of:', o: ['Chitin', 'Cellulose', 'Peptidoglycan', 'Silica'], a: 1, e: 'Cell wall mainly made of cellulose.' },
    { id: 'q137', p: ['pl2'], type: 'stmts', lvl: 1, q: 'Kingdom Plantae includes: A. Algae B. Bryophytes C. Fungi D. Gymnosperms', o: ['A, B and D', 'A, B, C and D', 'B and D only', 'A and C'], a: 0, e: 'Fungi form a separate kingdom.' },
    { id: 'q138', p: ['pl3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The phenomenon in which the diploid sporophytic and haploid gametophytic phases alternate is called:', o: ['Metagenesis of animals', 'Alternation of generations', 'Dikaryophase', 'Parthenogenesis'], a: 1, e: 'Alternation of generations.' },
    { id: 'q139', p: ['pl3'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Lengths of haploid and diploid phases are the same in all plant groups.', 'The gametophytic phase of plants is haploid.'], a: 3, e: 'They vary among different groups.' },
    { id: 'q140', p: ['an1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Animals store food reserves as:', o: ['Starch', 'Glycogen or fat', 'Cellulose', 'Floridean starch'], a: 1, e: 'They store food reserves as glycogen or fat.' },
    { id: 'q141', p: ['an1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Animals synthesise their own food from inorganic substances.', R: 'Animals are heterotrophic.' }, a: 3, e: 'Animals are heterotrophs, so A is false; R is true.' },
    { id: 'q142', p: ['an2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The mode of nutrition of animals, by ingestion of food, is:', o: ['Saprophytic', 'Holozoic', 'Autotrophic', 'Parasitic only'], a: 1, e: 'Holozoic – by ingestion of food.' },
    { id: 'q143', p: ['an2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Animals follow a definite growth pattern.', 'Sexual reproduction in animals is by copulation of male and female followed by embryological development.'], a: 0, e: 'Both true.' },
    { id: 'q144', p: ['vr1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Which are NOT mentioned in Whittaker\'s five kingdom classification?', o: ['Fungi', 'Viruses, viroids, prions and lichens', 'Protozoans', 'Bacteria'], a: 1, e: 'Lichens and acellular viruses, viroids, prions are not mentioned.' },
    { id: 'q145', p: ['vr1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Viruses did not find a place in classification.', R: 'Viruses are not considered truly living as they lack a cell structure.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q146', p: ['vr2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The word virus means:', o: ['Living fluid', 'Venom or poisonous fluid', 'Small body', 'Infectious protein'], a: 1, e: 'Virus means venom or poisonous fluid.' },
    { id: 'q147', p: ['vr2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Once a virus infects a cell, it:', o: ['Becomes dormant forever', 'Takes over the host cell machinery to replicate itself, killing the host', 'Starts photosynthesis', 'Divides by fission'], a: 1, e: 'They take over the machinery of the host cell to replicate.' },
    { id: 'q148', p: ['vr3'], type: 'match', lvl: 2, q: 'Match the scientist with the contribution.', match: { h: ['Scientist', 'Contribution'], l: ['D.J. Ivanowsky (1892)', 'M.W. Beijerinck (1898)', 'W.M. Stanley (1935)', 'T.O. Diener (1971)'], r: ['Discovered viroids', 'Viruses can be crystallised', 'Named "Contagium vivum fluidum"', 'Recognised causal organism of mosaic disease of tobacco'] }, o: ['A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I', 'A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I', 'A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I', 'A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I'], a: 0, e: 'Ivanowsky – TMV agent; Beijerinck – Contagium vivum fluidum; Stanley – crystallisation; Diener – viroids.' },
    { id: 'q149', p: ['vr3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Viruses were found to be smaller than bacteria because:', o: ['They were seen under light microscope', 'They passed through bacteria-proof filters', 'They could be crystallised', 'They had no nucleus'], a: 1, e: 'Ivanowsky found they passed through bacteria-proof filters.' },
    { id: 'q150', p: ['vr4'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'W.M. Stanley (1935) showed that virus crystals consist largely of:', o: ['Lipids', 'Proteins', 'Carbohydrates', 'DNA only'], a: 1, e: 'Crystals consist largely of proteins.' },
    { id: 'q151', p: ['vr4'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Viruses are inert outside their specific host cell.', 'Viruses are facultative parasites.'], a: 2, e: 'Viruses are obligate parasites.' },
    { id: 'q152', p: ['vr5'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Which statement about viral genetic material is correct?', o: ['All viruses contain both DNA and RNA', 'No virus contains both RNA and DNA', 'Viruses lack genetic material', 'Only animal viruses have DNA and RNA together'], a: 1, e: 'No virus contains both RNA and DNA.' },
    { id: 'q153', p: ['vr5'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'A virus is a nucleoprotein.', R: 'The protein of the virus is infectious, not its genetic material.' }, a: 2, e: 'The genetic material is infectious — R false.' },
    { id: 'q154', p: ['vr6'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Viruses that infect plants generally have:', o: ['Double stranded DNA', 'Single stranded RNA', 'Double stranded RNA', 'Single stranded DNA'], a: 1, e: 'Plant viruses have single stranded RNA.' },
    { id: 'q155', p: ['vr6'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Bacteriophages are usually:', o: ['Single stranded RNA viruses', 'Double stranded DNA viruses', 'Single stranded DNA viruses', 'Viroids'], a: 1, e: 'Bacterial viruses are usually double stranded DNA viruses.' },
    { id: 'q156', p: ['vr7'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The protein coat of a virus is called:', o: ['Capsomere', 'Capsid', 'Envelope', 'Pellicle'], a: 1, e: 'Capsid, made of small subunits called capsomeres.' },
    { id: 'q157', p: ['vr7'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Capsomeres are arranged in:', o: ['Helical or polyhedral geometric forms', 'Only circular forms', 'Random forms', 'Spiral thalli'], a: 0, e: 'Helical or polyhedral geometric forms.' },
    { id: 'q158', p: ['vr7'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Which part is NOT labelled in the bacteriophage of Fig 2.6?', o: ['Head', 'Collar', 'Tail fibres', 'Heterocyst'], a: 3, e: 'Bacteriophage: head, collar, sheath, tail fibres. Heterocyst is in Nostoc.' },
    { id: 'q159', p: ['vr8'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Which disease is NOT caused by a virus?', o: ['Mumps', 'Herpes', 'AIDS', 'Typhoid'], a: 3, e: 'Typhoid is bacterial.' },
    { id: 'q160', p: ['vr8'], type: 'stmts', lvl: 2, q: 'Symptoms of viral infection in plants include: A. Mosaic formation B. Leaf rolling and curling C. Vein clearing D. Dwarfing and stunted growth', o: ['A and B only', 'A, B and C only', 'B, C and D only', 'A, B, C and D'], a: 3, e: 'All are listed, along with yellowing.' },
    { id: 'q161', p: ['vd1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Viroids were discovered by:', o: ['Ivanowsky', 'Stanley', 'T.O. Diener', 'Beijerinck'], a: 2, e: 'T.O. Diener in 1971.' },
    { id: 'q162', p: ['vd1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Viroids cause:', o: ['Mad cow disease', 'Potato spindle tuber disease', 'Tobacco mosaic disease', 'Citrus canker'], a: 1, e: 'Potato spindle tuber disease.' },
    { id: 'q163', p: ['vd1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'The infectious agent discovered by Diener was named viroid.', R: 'It was a free RNA lacking the protein coat found in viruses.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains the name.' },
    { id: 'q164', p: ['pn1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Prions consist of:', o: ['Free RNA', 'Abnormally folded protein', 'DNA and protein', 'Lipid'], a: 1, e: 'Agent consisting of abnormally folded protein.' },
    { id: 'q165', p: ['pn1'], type: 'match', lvl: 2, q: 'Match the disease with its causative agent.', match: { h: ['Disease', 'Agent'], l: ['Potato spindle tuber', 'BSE (mad cow disease)', 'Tobacco mosaic', 'Citrus canker'], r: ['Bacterium', 'Virus', 'Prion', 'Viroid'] }, o: ['A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I', 'A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I', 'A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I', 'A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I'], a: 0, e: 'PSTD – viroid; BSE – prion; TMV – virus; citrus canker – bacterium.' },
    { id: 'q166', p: ['pn1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Creutzfeldt–Jacob disease (CJD) in humans is caused by:', o: ['Viroids', 'Prions', 'Bacteria', 'Protozoa'], a: 1, e: 'CJD is the analogous human variant of BSE, caused by prions.' },
    { id: 'q167', p: ['lc1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'In lichens, the algal component is called:', o: ['Mycobiont', 'Phycobiont', 'Heterocyst', 'Hypha'], a: 1, e: 'Algal component – phycobiont; fungal – mycobiont.' },
    { id: 'q168', p: ['lc1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Mycobiont is autotrophic.', 'Phycobiont is autotrophic.'], a: 3, e: 'Mycobiont (fungus) is heterotrophic.' },
    { id: 'q169', p: ['lc2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'In a lichen, the fungal partner:', o: ['Prepares food for algae', 'Provides shelter and absorbs mineral nutrients and water', 'Fixes CO2', 'Releases toxins'], a: 1, e: 'Algae prepare food; fungi provide shelter and absorb minerals and water.' },
    { id: 'q170', p: ['lc2', 'lc1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Lichens are a mutually useful association.', R: 'Both the alga and the fungus benefit from each other.' }, a: 0, e: 'Symbiotic, mutually useful association.' },
    { id: 'q171', p: ['lc3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Lichens are very good indicators of:', o: ['Soil fertility', 'Air pollution', 'Water hardness', 'Temperature'], a: 1, e: 'Lichens are very good pollution indicators.' },
    { id: 'q172', p: ['lc3'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Lichens grow abundantly in highly polluted areas.', R: 'Lichens do not grow in polluted areas.' }, a: 3, e: 'Lichens do not grow in polluted areas, so A is false; R is true.' }
  ]
});
